1
00:00:00,500 --> 00:00:05,558
Today your chip learns three things: to feel a button, to dim a light, and to

2
00:00:05,558 --> 00:00:06,740
measure a voltage.

3
00:00:07,740 --> 00:00:13,202
A chip's metal connections are called pins. Many pins can do several jobs, like a

4
00:00:13,202 --> 00:00:17,180
person with many hats. The pin mux setting chooses the hat.

5
00:00:17,680 --> 00:00:20,720
An input pin listens. An output pin acts.

6
00:00:21,720 --> 00:00:26,388
Our button's wire is gently held high by a pull-up, like a spring holding a door

7
00:00:26,388 --> 00:00:26,680
shut.

8
00:00:27,180 --> 00:00:33,235
Press the button and the wire connects to ground: it goes low. The exact moment it

9
00:00:33,235 --> 00:00:36,780
drops from high to low is called a falling edge.

10
00:00:37,280 --> 00:00:38,880
Pressed means high or low?

11
00:00:39,380 --> 00:00:44,380
Your turn. Pause the video.

12
00:00:44,380 --> 00:00:45,420
Low.

13
00:00:46,420 --> 00:00:52,226
One way to notice a press is to keep asking. That's polling. It works, but you can't

14
00:00:52,226 --> 00:00:55,060
do much else. A better way is a doorbell.

15
00:00:55,560 --> 00:01:00,808
The hardware interrupts your program when something happens. A tiny function answers

16
00:01:00,808 --> 00:01:04,120
the door, leaves a note called a flag, and goes back.

17
00:01:04,620 --> 00:01:08,140
The main program reads the note and does the work.

18
00:01:09,140 --> 00:01:13,997
If two doorbells ring together, priority numbers decide. On this chip, the smaller

19
00:01:13,997 --> 00:01:15,300
number is more urgent.

20
00:01:15,800 --> 00:01:19,800
In our dashboard, the button is one and the timer is two.

21
00:01:20,800 --> 00:01:26,686
Metal inside a button bounces when it closes, like a dropped ball. The chip is so

22
00:01:26,686 --> 00:01:29,520
fast it may count one press as several.

23
00:01:30,020 --> 00:01:35,290
The cure is debouncing: notice the first touch, stop listening briefly, wait a

24
00:01:35,290 --> 00:01:40,020
moment, check the button is still down, count once, then listen again.

25
00:01:41,020 --> 00:01:46,875
A basic light output is only on or off. So how do we get half bright? Blink it so

26
00:01:46,875 --> 00:01:49,260
fast your eyes blend it together.

27
00:01:49,760 --> 00:01:55,641
The share of time it's on is the duty cycle. Ten percent looks dim. Ninety percent

28
00:01:55,641 --> 00:02:00,160
looks bright. This trick is called PWM, pulse width modulation.

29
00:02:00,660 --> 00:02:06,980
On our board, low means on, so a longer low time looks brighter.

30
00:02:07,480 --> 00:02:11,965
A hardware timer makes the pulses by itself, twenty-four thousand per second, while

31
00:02:11,965 --> 00:02:13,640
your program does other things.

32
00:02:14,140 --> 00:02:19,299
Curious about the numbers? With a timer ticking forty-eight million times a second,

33
00:02:19,299 --> 00:02:22,780
it counts up to a thousand and back down for each blink.

34
00:02:23,280 --> 00:02:27,996
Twenty-five percent means the switching point is two hundred fifty. The helper code

35
00:02:27,996 --> 00:02:29,360
calculates this for you.

36
00:02:30,360 --> 00:02:36,840
The world is smooth: light, heat, a turning knob. The chip likes whole numbers.

37
00:02:37,340 --> 00:02:43,787
The ADC, analog to digital converter, measures a voltage and gives a number from

38
00:02:43,787 --> 00:02:48,380
zero to four thousand ninety-five. Zero means zero volts.

39
00:02:48,880 --> 00:02:52,000
The top number means about three point three volts.

40
00:02:52,500 --> 00:02:56,580
If the number is about two thousand forty-eight, roughly what voltage is that?

41
00:02:57,080 --> 00:03:02,080
Your turn. Pause the video.

42
00:03:02,080 --> 00:03:07,120
About half: 1.65 volts, or 1650 millivolts.

43
00:03:08,120 --> 00:03:13,547
To get millivolts, multiply by three thousand three hundred first, then divide.

44
00:03:13,547 --> 00:03:19,249
Whole-number division throws away leftovers, so dividing first would leave you with

45
00:03:19,249 --> 00:03:20,280
almost nothing.

46
00:03:21,280 --> 00:03:27,236
Doorbells for buttons, fast blinking for brightness, and a number ruler for voltage.

47
00:03:27,236 --> 00:03:30,640
Next you'll build each one. See you in demo one.

